        "name": "Giovanni Fruzzetti",
        "sort_order": 87269,
        "photo": null,
        "description": "Giovanni Fruzzetti was born 1865 in Carrara, Italy. Immigrated to the United States in 1892.\n\nLived in Barre, Vermont, until 1905, where he was a friend of Luigi Galleani and helped found Cronaca Sovversiva. Later lived in Quincy, Massachusetts, before purchasing a sixty-acre farm in Bridgewater, Massachusetts, in 1910. Married with ten children and also supported six nieces and nephews following the death of his sister-in-law. Suffered ongoing health problems stemming from an appendicitis operation. Arrested in May 1918, Fruzzetti stated his belief “in the overthrow of any government by force.” He also emphasized the hardship deportation would impose on his family, noting that his wife could not return to Italy because of their disabled American-born daughter. Despite this, he was deported on June 24, 1919.\n\nUnable to make a living in Italy, he received financial assistance from comrades in the United States until his death in 1926.\n\nAdapted from Kenyon Zimmer, Red Scare Deportees (kenyonzimmer.com), compiled from INS deportation case files.",
